# ws/memfd-default — sealed memfd data segments become the default
#
# Change: the data segment is now an anonymous sealed memfd by default. The
# worker creates it once (memfd_create + ftruncate + MADV_HUGEPAGE +
# F_SEAL_SHRINK|F_SEAL_GROW), hands the descriptor to the client over the ring's
# AF_UNIX SOCK_SEQPACKET channel (SCM_RIGHTS), and both sides keep the mapping
# for the life of the worker/process. FASTPOSTGRES_MEMFD=0 (or off/false/no)
# restores the named /dev/shm segment, which is also the automatic fallback if
# the listener/handoff or the kernel's memfd/seals are unavailable.
#
# The handoff socket is authenticated with SO_PEERCRED on both sides: the worker
# only sends descriptors to the ring's client pid, and the client only accepts
# the worker pid it published before worker_ready.
#
# The eventfd engine (opt-in) does not run the per-worker handoff, so with memfd
# on it now says so once and uses /dev/shm up front, instead of waiting for a
# connection that never comes.
#
# Baseline is the fixed fpq-baseline image (main@7e3d2e6) per the protocol.
# This branch is based on merge/transport, so the A/B below includes that merge
# as well as the memfd change; a same-host control against fpq-transport
# (merge/transport) is included to isolate this change.
#
# Every run was made under /tmp/fpq-bench.lock, one at a time, same command.

## Build (change image, from this worktree)

    flock /tmp/fpq-bench.lock -c 'docker build -q --target dev -t fpq-memfd-default -f docker/Dockerfile .'
    -> sha256:a804bbe683068644cea65e189e30dff7718a45480397c30543fb653780ac89d8

## Commands

100K:

    flock /tmp/fpq-bench.lock -c 'docker run --rm --shm-size=3g -e BENCH_GIT_REV=baseline        fpq-baseline        benchmarks.sh 100000'
    flock /tmp/fpq-bench.lock -c 'docker run --rm --shm-size=3g -e BENCH_GIT_REV=memfd-default   fpq-memfd-default   benchmarks.sh 100000'

3K:

    flock /tmp/fpq-bench.lock -c 'docker run --rm --shm-size=3g -e BENCH_GIT_REV=baseline        fpq-baseline        benchmarks.sh 3000'
    flock /tmp/fpq-bench.lock -c 'docker run --rm --shm-size=3g -e BENCH_GIT_REV=memfd-default   fpq-memfd-default   benchmarks.sh 3000'

Control, same host, merge/transport image without this change (default /dev/shm):

    flock /tmp/fpq-bench.lock -c 'docker run --rm --shm-size=3g -e BENCH_GIT_REV=transport       fpq-transport       benchmarks.sh 3000'
    flock /tmp/fpq-bench.lock -c 'docker run --rm --shm-size=3g -e BENCH_GIT_REV=transport       fpq-transport       benchmarks.sh 100000'

Three alternating repetitions per image and size for baseline/change; two for
the transport control. The tables quote medians; every per-run value is listed
so the spread travels with the number. The host carried background load during
this window (the same cells move several percent run to run, and two runs took
over 2 minutes against ~25 s for the same command).

