Against the transport control (the branch's actual base), the medians overlap:

    100K change vs transport: stream() 9.02M vs 9.03M, DBAPI 7.64M vs 7.85M,
    core 7.49M vs 7.66M, 4 processes 23.44M vs 23.07M, Arrow 2.34M vs 2.30M.
    3K change vs transport:   stream() 10.89M vs 9.20M (transport's own two runs
    were 7.17M and 11.22M), DBAPI 10.62M vs 11.15M, core 5.21M vs 5.78M, 4
    processes 4.35M vs 4.18M.

So the default memfd costs nothing measurable on either size: every changed
cell is inside this host's run-to-run spread, and the large baseline->change
gains belong to the merge/transport work this branch builds on. The small-batch
3K read stays below baseline latency, where the data-segment handoff is a
larger fraction of the work.

## Correctness gate

    flock /tmp/fpq-bench.lock -c 'docker run --rm --shm-size=3g fpq-memfd-default tests.sh'
    -> 61 tests: OK (skipped=1); extension pg_regress: 1/1 passed

    flock /tmp/fpq-bench.lock -c 'docker run --rm --shm-size=3g -e FASTPOSTGRES_MEMFD=1 fpq-memfd-default tests.sh'
    -> 61 tests: OK (skipped=1)

    flock /tmp/fpq-bench.lock -c 'docker run --rm --shm-size=3g -e FASTPOSTGRES_TRANSPORT=eventfd fpq-memfd-default tests.sh'
    -> 61 tests: OK (0 skipped); memfd+eventfd emits the one/runtime warning
       "memfd data segments are not supported by the eventfd engine; using
       /dev/shm" and reads correctly over the named segment.

The two tests that inspect /dev/shm/<seg> (test_the_data_segment_capacity_is_
the_clients_ask, test_parallel.test_threads_each_get_their_own_partition) now
set FASTPOSTGRES_MEMFD=0 for themselves; they were asserting the named file,
which the default deliberately does not create. test_memfd covers the new path:
no /dev/shm file, F_SEAL_SHRINK|F_SEAL_GROW present, both ftruncate directions
refused with EPERM.
